WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:02.910
Hi there, Steve Kaufmann here.

00:00:03.180 --> 00:00:07.650
Today I wanna talk about something
important, uh, maybe more important than

00:00:08.029 --> 00:00:13.530
language learning, and it's learning,
learning throughout our lives, learning as

00:00:13.530 --> 00:00:20.190
adults, learning as people who are growing
older, learning as a major driver of

00:00:20.190 --> 00:00:24.390
health, of, uh, happiness, uh, of success.

00:00:25.290 --> 00:00:29.970
And the reason I'm talking about that is
I just finished listening to an audiobook

00:00:29.970 --> 00:00:36.480
in German by my favorite, uh, German
neuroscientist, Manfred Spitzer, uh, where

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:41.280
he speaks with another gentleman whose
name was Norbert Hershkowitz, and they

00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:47.110
talk about ... how adults think and learn.

00:00:47.849 --> 00:00:53.879
And there are three books and the
last one in particular ... living

00:00:53.879 --> 00:01:03.120
throughout your life, uh, 60 years
plus ... like how old people think.

00:01:03.210 --> 00:01:05.580
And there's a lot of very
interesting information there.

00:01:05.580 --> 00:01:09.780
I'll leave a link to the book, uh, if
you want to find it, if you understand

00:01:10.050 --> 00:01:13.860
German, but it got me to thinking
about this whole issue of learning.

00:01:13.860 --> 00:01:18.060
In fact, I, I focused my discussions
here on, on language learning and then

00:01:18.060 --> 00:01:22.395
my interest in language earning and
tips that I have based on my experience.

00:01:23.024 --> 00:01:27.225
But you know, I realized that
that learning I is a huge

00:01:27.375 --> 00:01:29.024
issue in our lives today.

00:01:29.175 --> 00:01:34.274
Governments spend a fortune
on education and health.

00:01:34.304 --> 00:01:41.955
Those two items typically account for 20%
of, uh, GDP in most developed countries.

00:01:42.585 --> 00:01:47.024
Uh, learning though is something
that depends on the individual,

00:01:47.054 --> 00:01:51.225
not on how much money the
government or other people spend.

00:01:51.675 --> 00:01:57.285
I should add in, in terms of this
whole learning thing um, when I see

00:01:57.464 --> 00:02:01.095
the, the amount of student debt that
people accumulate, especially in the

00:02:01.100 --> 00:02:05.895
United States, it kind of puts them
at a great disadvantage, although

00:02:05.895 --> 00:02:10.995
statistically they'll end up earning more
and, uh, probably have other benefits.

00:02:11.745 --> 00:02:17.160
So learning is a major issue
and never before has it been

00:02:17.160 --> 00:02:19.980
so easy to learn things.

00:02:20.220 --> 00:02:21.720
I mean, people have always learned.

00:02:21.720 --> 00:02:24.360
They've learned by
watching people do things.

00:02:24.360 --> 00:02:27.990
They've learned by trial and
error, doing things themselves.

00:02:28.380 --> 00:02:34.200
But reading and listening, like the audio
book that I just mentioned is a very

00:02:34.430 --> 00:02:37.500
quick and easy way of acquiring knowledge.

00:02:37.830 --> 00:02:41.400
And whenever we're acquiring
knowledge, our brains are changing

00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:43.410
and growing and remaining young.

00:02:44.130 --> 00:02:47.310
And, uh, so it is important to
take advantage of these things.

00:02:47.790 --> 00:02:52.260
The ability to take advantage of
these things relies on our curiosity.

00:02:52.829 --> 00:02:57.780
Uh, again, Manfred Spitzer, uh, in
his, uh, audiobook, he references

00:02:57.785 --> 00:03:01.920
Einstein who said, I have no more
talent than anyone else, is just

00:03:01.920 --> 00:03:04.079
that I am passionately curious.

00:03:04.410 --> 00:03:06.480
So curiosity is the driver.

00:03:06.690 --> 00:03:09.060
Curiosity is why I have
so many books behind me.

00:03:09.060 --> 00:03:12.960
This is only one of seven big
bookshelves that I have in my house.

00:03:13.365 --> 00:03:17.415
I'm interested in things and it's
important to remain interested in things.

00:03:18.315 --> 00:03:23.385
Sometimes when people talk about
learning, they overly complicate things.

00:03:23.385 --> 00:03:29.115
So I did some research, uh, using
Google, uh, and I just punched in there

00:03:29.120 --> 00:03:35.834
how old people think and learn, uh,
learning and aging and so forth, and a

00:03:35.834 --> 00:03:42.720
lot of the sort of advice that's given
is, you know, over complicating, like

00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:46.110
older people, they prefer repetition.

00:03:46.110 --> 00:03:50.160
Well, repetition has its place in
education, whether you're old or young.

00:03:50.610 --> 00:03:54.060
Uh, older people are more
self-directed, says one.

00:03:54.060 --> 00:03:56.220
I don't see that necessarily
as being the case.

00:03:56.730 --> 00:04:00.960
Um, again, it is not clear
who they mean by older.

00:04:01.500 --> 00:04:05.190
Uh, there's reference to the
number of neurons we lose.

00:04:05.250 --> 00:04:08.160
Obviously there's a sort of
an evolution in our brains.

00:04:08.595 --> 00:04:12.105
But, uh, Spitzer points out, we
have so many neurons that we can

00:04:12.105 --> 00:04:13.395
afford to lose quite a lot of them.

00:04:13.395 --> 00:04:15.045
We do lose some over time.

00:04:15.255 --> 00:04:16.665
It doesn't really affect anything.

00:04:17.085 --> 00:04:22.875
Uh, perhaps we have, you know, uh,
weaker memories at a certain point.

00:04:23.115 --> 00:04:27.435
But the point is, if you continue
learning, you will strengthen

00:04:27.435 --> 00:04:29.605
all of those cognitive functions.

00:04:29.835 --> 00:04:33.705
But here again, one of these articles
that I came up with, uh, I might leave

00:04:33.705 --> 00:04:37.575
these if you're interested, I can leave
some links to them, sort of applies that

00:04:38.475 --> 00:04:43.365
a college education is very important
to your mental health as you grow older.

00:04:43.695 --> 00:04:47.325
Implication being that someone
who has had a college education

00:04:47.325 --> 00:04:48.945
has learned so many things.

00:04:49.245 --> 00:04:52.665
Perhaps they're more curious about
things, they will continue learning.

00:04:52.665 --> 00:04:56.805
They've got a base that's gonna help
them, but nothing prevents a person

00:04:56.805 --> 00:05:02.025
who doesn't have a college education
from being equally curious and, you

00:05:02.025 --> 00:05:06.135
know, reading and listening, and
keeping their brains stimulated.

00:05:06.955 --> 00:05:11.565
Spitzer also makes the point that it's
not just a matter of keeping your brain

00:05:11.565 --> 00:05:16.905
active, like doing Sudoku, because
Sudoku is not bringing anything new in,

00:05:17.325 --> 00:05:21.105
and it's very important, I've seen this
elsewhere, that you bring in things that

00:05:21.105 --> 00:05:27.315
are new, you discover new things that
change you in a way, and that by, you

00:05:27.315 --> 00:05:34.215
know, processing this new information,
then, uh, you are maintaining the, the

00:05:34.335 --> 00:05:39.419
youth and the, the activity of your, of
your brain, uh, which is very important.

00:05:40.080 --> 00:05:41.609
Uh, of course, other things...

00:05:41.609 --> 00:05:43.530
being active is very good for your brain.

00:05:43.890 --> 00:05:45.299
Uh, jogging is good for your brain.

00:05:45.299 --> 00:05:48.180
So many things are good for your brain,
but it, it, I don't know that we need

00:05:48.180 --> 00:05:51.479
to think of it always in terms of doing
it because it's good for our brains.

00:05:51.870 --> 00:05:56.400
If we are genuinely curious, then
we derive tremendous satisfaction

00:05:56.460 --> 00:05:59.609
from learning about other places.

00:06:00.030 --> 00:06:00.629
Learning...

00:06:01.400 --> 00:06:03.990
travel is a form of
learning about other places.

00:06:04.755 --> 00:06:08.055
Learning languages, of course, which
is what I'm doing now, learning

00:06:08.325 --> 00:06:10.155
Polish because I'm going to Poland.

00:06:10.155 --> 00:06:12.435
I mean, I did have some
previous exposure to Polish.

00:06:13.094 --> 00:06:18.465
Uh, so it's this curiosity rather than
how many years we've spent at school.

00:06:19.034 --> 00:06:23.745
And also, again, in some of these, um,
articles, I found there was a great

00:06:23.745 --> 00:06:28.245
reliance on these theories of, uh,
different kinds of learners that older

00:06:28.245 --> 00:06:32.265
people might be more visual learners
or might be more kinetic learners.

00:06:32.265 --> 00:06:35.730
And, of course that whole theory
of different types of learners has

00:06:35.880 --> 00:06:41.280
largely been debunked, and I think
we're guided more by our curiosity.

00:06:41.730 --> 00:06:46.290
Uh, some people don't like to read, uh,
which is fine, but the way the brain

00:06:46.295 --> 00:06:50.970
learns is essentially the same, uh, with
all people, and that's more or less become

00:06:51.360 --> 00:06:58.290
accepted as the fact and these efforts
by educators to categorize people as this

00:06:58.290 --> 00:07:00.450
kind of learner or that kind of learner.

00:07:00.450 --> 00:07:03.210
Or if you're a young learner, then
it's this way and if you're an

00:07:03.210 --> 00:07:04.770
older learner, it's some other way.

00:07:05.340 --> 00:07:08.280
Uh, I'm not, uh, impressed with those.

00:07:08.280 --> 00:07:14.730
I think, uh, I prefer the simple position
that I may have lost some neurons, but as

00:07:14.730 --> 00:07:19.740
long as I'm curious, if I keep discovering
new things, learning new things, reading

00:07:19.740 --> 00:07:25.260
books, learning languages, it's gonna
keep my brain as young as it can be,

00:07:25.265 --> 00:07:27.270
given the fact that I'm 77 years old.

00:07:27.705 --> 00:07:29.295
So, there you have it.

00:07:29.415 --> 00:07:32.565
I just wanted to sort of broaden,
because I always talk about language

00:07:32.565 --> 00:07:36.645
learning, but actually I'm learning i,
I'm interested in education as a whole.

00:07:36.915 --> 00:07:41.805
I read quite a bit on the subject
and I think it's a vital part of

00:07:42.585 --> 00:07:49.305
being, you know, fully engaged as
human beings, uh, at whatever age.

00:07:49.725 --> 00:07:51.075
So there you have it.

00:07:51.285 --> 00:07:53.805
Just a little bit of a digression
from language learning.

00:07:53.810 --> 00:07:55.215
I hope you found that interesting.

00:07:55.485 --> 00:07:56.745
I look forward to your comments.

00:07:57.090 --> 00:07:57.780
Bye for now.

